**Smart pointers (RAII for ownership).**
- `std::unique_ptr<T>`: sole owner, move-only, zero overhead; the default choice. Created with `std::make_unique`.
- `std::shared_ptr<T>`: shared ownership via an atomic reference count; the object is destroyed when the last owner goes. Use `make_shared`. Beware of cycles.
- `std::weak_ptr<T>`: non-owning observer of a shared_ptr; breaks cycles and is checked with `lock()`.

**Friend.** `friend class B;` lets B access A's private members. It breaks encapsulation selectively, so use it for tightly coupled helpers (factories, test fixtures, operators). Friendship is not inherited and not mutual.

**Templates.** A function or class template is instantiated at compile time per type, giving type-safe generic code with no runtime cost. Know specialization, that definitions normally live in headers, and that errors appear at instantiation.

**Correct Singleton (Meyers singleton).**

```cpp
class Logger {
public:
    static Logger& instance() {
        static Logger inst;          // initialised once, thread-safe since C++11
        return inst;
    }
    Logger(const Logger&) = delete;
    Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete;
    Logger(Logger&&) = delete;
    Logger& operator=(Logger&&) = delete;
    void log(const std::string& m);
private:
    Logger() = default;
    ~Logger() = default;
};
```

Why it is correct: the constructor is private; copy and move are deleted so no second instance can be made; returning a reference prevents accidental `delete`; C++11 guarantees that initialisation of a function-local static happens exactly once even with concurrent callers. Common mistakes: returning a raw pointer from `new` that never gets freed, a hand-written double-checked lock without atomics (a data race), and forgetting to delete copying.

**Trade-offs.** Singletons are global state: they hide dependencies, make unit testing hard, and static destruction order can bite if other statics use it at exit. Prefer dependency injection unless a single instance is a real requirement.
